    24|{"id": "fs_162", "fact": "Bia hơi cốc — the humble handmade blue-green beer glass of Hanoi (Vietnam) has survived 50+ years unchanged through war, socialist austerity, market reforms, and globalization. Designed during the subsidy era (post-1975) by the Vietnamese Central Cooperative Union of Handicrafts and Crafts as a standardized single-serving glass for Bia hơi (fresh beer), each glass is individually hand-blown from recycled glass in small village factories. Despite China's mass-produced crystal products flooding the market, no manufacturer has replicated the low-priced, 'unpretty' cốc. The glass embodies the tension between sameness and difference in Vietnam's transformation, and challenges tech-era narratives of constant optimization — as the article says: 'its persistence points to how the unassuming forces of habit and utility can hold ground against betterment and efficiency.' Featured in The Sunday Long Read (May 15, 2026), 11 points on HN.", "source": "https://sundaylongread.com/2026/05/15/hanois-humble-beer-glass-and-the-memory-of-a-nation/ (HN 11pts, May 19 2026)", "date": "2026-05-19", "tags": "persistent,culture,design,vietnam,anthropology,material-culture,slowness", "confidence": 0.9}
